contentText:
During P1Harmony &#8216;s first six years together, there was a stretch when members couldn&#8217;t articulate why they were working so hard.

&#8220;What I felt was hardest in this process was when I couldn&#8217;t find a reason for running,&#8221; Intak tells Billboard during a sit-down in their downtown Los Angeles hotel hours before they fly home to Seoul. &#8220;There were times the company just had us run without a clear purpose or a point to reach and I think I got a little worn out the moment I realized that. &#8216;What is our purpose in this moment? What are we running this hard for? Is it even true that running like this will get us there?&#8217; Every time I had moments like that, I&#8217;d suddenly feel drained and it was hard. But even if our steps were really small, we always climbed little by little.&#8221;

Six years into their career, the six-piece of Intak, Keeho, Theo, Jiung, Soul and Jongseob has a much clearer view of where all that running is taking them. The K-pop act&#8217;s newest EP BLOW UP arrived Friday (Nov. 6), and its title doubles as a mantra for where the group stands today. After years of steady gains on the charts, in album sales, in concert audiences and in hard-won lessons inside the boy band, they&#8217;re ready to break through in a bigger way.

P1Harmony’s performance at 82WORLD: LA , their first headlining slot at a U.S. festival the previous weekend, felt like proof. &#8220;The last time we did an outdoor thing was the first time we came to L.A.,&#8221; leader Keeho recollects. &#8220;It&#8217;s been like almost four to five years and that&#8217;s a really big, full-circle moment.&#8221;

Numbers show how far that gratitude goes, with Intak saying he can track the group&#8217;s progress in chart positions. &#8220;Before, our goal was just to get onto Billboard ,&#8221; he says. &#8220;Just the other day we were at No. 60, then we&#8217;re getting into the top 20 and now the top 5. Because we achieved these things one by one, I feel reassured now and I think it&#8217;s good that we can show our fans we&#8217;re artists who can do at least this much.&#8221;

P1H&#8217;s six albums’ worth of Billboard 200 history &#8212; all EPs &#8212; show just that: After first entering the chart at No. 51 with HARMONY : ALL IN during the summer of 2023, P1Harmony earned their first top 40 album with 2024&#8217;s Killin&#8217; It (No. 39 peak), followed by SAD SONG (No. 16 in 2024), DUH! (No. 23 in 2025) and their debut English-language EP, EX (No. 9, their first Top 10 in fall 2025). Earlier this year, UNIQUE debuted at No. 4 on the Billboard 200 , the group&#8217;s best showing yet, earning 58,000 equivalent album units in its first week.

While they&#8217;re aiming even higher with BLOW UP , bigger shifts have been happening behind the scenes.

&#8220;Because K-pop seems very exotic, people think it&#8217;s like, &#8216;Whoa, these guys have no control over their careers and whatnot,'&#8221; Toronto native Keeho says of outside perceptions. &#8220;That is the case for some people, I will not lie, but I feel like even labels out here [in the U.S.] are really controlling with the creatives you work with, the people you&#8217;re allowed to talk to, the visuals and stuff like that — they won&#8217;t listen to you unless you make it big. It&#8217;s the same thing with us.&#8221;

What changed the conversation with P1Harmony&#8217;s Korean label and agency, FNC Entertainment, was evidence, perhaps best seen when the Keeho-composed album track &#8220;Pretty Boy&#8221; off DUH! became an unexpected breakout streaming hit, earning 12 million more listens on Spotify than the album&#8217;s title track single despite no promotion tied to it.

&#8220;The company sees successes like &#8216;Pretty Boy&#8217; and the fans liking the music that the members are making, taking direction in, [creating] choreo for, the stage direction that Theo takes,” explains Keeho. “It&#8217;s not just, &#8216;Hey, listen to us&#8217; without any evidence; it&#8217;s the company saying, &#8216;Hey, actually the members are taking directions in their own fields and the fans are loving it — maybe we should listen to them more.&#8217; I think it&#8217;s that buildup that now, finally, in [year six], they&#8217;re like, &#8216;Hey, maybe y&#8217;all actually know what you guys are talking about.'&#8221;

For UNIQUE this year, the group thought ahead about what other songs might connect with audiences.

&#8220;We really communicated to the company that something might hit differently, so let&#8217;s prepare,&#8221; Keeho continues, sharing that the sextet landed on using their budget to make visuals for “L.O.Y.L.” (because “artistically, that&#8217;s something we&#8217;ve never done”) and “ICE (VVS)” (&#8220;&#8216;UNIQUE&#8217; is gonna have this moment, but people at the end of the day are gonna listen to &#8216;ICE&#8217; — it&#8217;s going to be the one&#8221;). True to their vision: the most-listened songs from the record on Spotify are &#8220;UNIQUE&#8221; (15.5 million streams), &#8220;ICE (VVS)&#8221; (14.5 million) and &#8220;L.O.Y.L.&#8221; (6.9 million).

That track record carried into the choice of &#8220;BLOW UP&#8221; as P1H&#8217;s pick for a new single.

&#8220;We don&#8217;t get to call the shots in everything by any means, especially with our lead single,&#8221; Keeho says. &#8220;But I think it&#8217;s more like we can manipulate the system a little bit better now. It&#8217;d be more like, &#8216;Actually, I think this song&#8217;s a little better&#8217; and we can really communicate our thoughts and ideas. But after that, it&#8217;s kind of up in the air.&#8221;

&#8220;We&#8217;ve never done [anything] this rhythmical,&#8221; Jongseob, the group&#8217;s youngest, adds. &#8220;I get more of a youthful [feeling]. This time is for a new chapter and challenging something new. That&#8217;s why we especially think &#8216;BLOW UP&#8217; is better than the other options. We love &#8216;BLOW UP.'&#8221;

With TikTok-ready lip-sync lines sprinkled throughout the hyperpop track (&#8220;We vibin&#8217;, we feinin&#8217;, we mobbin'&#8221;), the song captures youthful ambition. Still, Intak, who has writing credits on every song on the EP alongside Jongseob, wanted the lyrics to show a harder, more mature edge .

&#8220;When we first heard &#8216;BLOW UP,&#8217; a lot of the keywords the members picked out first were about youth,&#8221; the 23-year-old shares. &#8220;Rather than the bright side people see, I wanted to reveal more of the painful side hidden behind it. So, with the lyrics, I thought of it as walking a tightrope. Some people take a plane or a helicopter or an escalator to get somewhere higher. But I thought of us as walking a tightrope, climbing so cautiously it feels like we could fall, and wrote the flow of the song with that in mind.&#8221;

When it comes to P1Harmony’s aim to reach the top of the pop charts, the work they did within the group was just as necessary as the work they do for fans and audiences.

For Keeho, who grew up in Canada, it started with the culture shock of training in Korea. &#8220;The level of discipline that is taught in Korea was not something I was used to at all,&#8221; he says. &#8220;Doing like 10-hour, 11-hour workdays as a kid and even as a trainee, it&#8217;s like, &#8216;Whoa.&#8217; You have to compromise your dignity almost a little bit — you have to kind of let them yell at you and be like, &#8216;I&#8217;m still gonna show you why I belong here.&#8217; But I wasn&#8217;t raised like that.&#8221;

Soul, who moved from his native Japan to train in Korea, says he went in with his eyes and mind open. &#8220;When I decided to do this work, I already knew it would be really hard and came in prepared for it.&#8221; Today, the homesickness he felt is, he adds in English, &#8220;no more.&#8221;

Theo&#8217;s compromise is musical. &#8220;I love an indie band, guitar-type of music,&#8221; he admits, before…
